    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ismailism">Ismaili</a> Shia <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a> born of a secret missionary network in North <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#africa">Africa</a>, set up in 910 as a rival to Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#baghdad">Baghdad</a>.</li><li>From <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a>, which it founded, it controlled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#red-sea">Red Sea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> trade and built al-Azhar, still a leading centre of Islamic learning.</li><li>Famine, army factions and a schism in 1094 eroded it until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saladin">Saladin</a> quietly restored Abbasid prayers in 1171.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ismailism">Ismailis</a> who broke away after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nizari-mustali-schism">1094 succession crisis</a> gave rise to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#imam">imams</a> known today as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aga-khan">Aga Khans</a>, a lineage tracing back to the Fatimid missionary network.</p></aside>
    </section>

